 "title": "Notes at the back",
 "brief": "A short, uneven stack of books, each one turned a little against the one below, the way books pile up on a desk that is actually used. Their covers take the theme's categorical hues, softened toward the page, so the stack reads as a small library rather than a monument. Around it, a few pale slips of paper circle slowly at different heights and speeds, like the notes Montaigne writes at the back of each book so that he will remember what he thought of it. Each slip is tied to the stack by a faint thread that slackens and tightens as it moves: judgement kept close to the thing judged.\n\nOne slip lags behind another, following it on a spring, the way one reading follows an earlier one. Fine dust turns in a wide ring at the height of the stack: memory, which Montaigne says fails him, held together only by the notes. Warm light from above, slow motion, a lot of quiet room.",
